# Rust & Tauri Standards

## Error Handling

Avoid `unwrap()` on anything that can realistically fail at runtime.

```rust
// ❌ BAD
let window = app.get_webview_window("main").unwrap();

// ✅ GOOD
let window = app.get_webview_window("main")
    .ok_or("main window not found")?;
```

Return `Result<T, String>` (or a typed error) from all Tauri commands so the frontend can handle failures.

## Mutex / State

- Use `lock().unwrap()` only where a panic is acceptable (startup/teardown)
- For runtime paths, prefer `.lock().map_err(|e| e.to_string())?`
- Never hold a mutex guard across an `await` point

## Transactional Hosts Write

Apply hosts **before** persisting to disk. If `apply_to_hosts` fails, roll back in-memory state and return an error — do not persist a state that doesn't match the system file.

```rust
// Correct order
apply_to_hosts(&contexts)?;   // fails → nothing persisted
save_contexts(&contexts)?;    // only runs if apply succeeded
```

## Input Validation

Validate IP addresses and hostnames before writing to the hosts file. Reject malformed entries with a descriptive error returned to the frontend.

## Serialization

- Use `serde_json::from_str(...).unwrap_or_default()` only when data loss is explicitly acceptable and logged
- Prefer returning an error so the UI can warn the user about corrupted storage

## Commands

- Every `#[tauri::command]` must have a doc-comment explaining its purpose and arguments
- Keep command functions thin — delegate to domain modules (`hosts.rs`, `storage.rs`, etc.)

## Security

- Do not construct shell commands with user-supplied content
- Validate any path received from the frontend before filesystem operations
- Keep `csp` configured in `tauri.conf.json`; avoid `null`
